  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 PM EST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nWith the high amplitude ridge sitting over the central CONUS\nwith a blocky-ish Omega flow pattern and extending well \nnorthward into central Canada, the northerly flow remains over \nthe area and has continued to bring lake effect clouds \ndownstream of the lake. With the slow progression of the ridge \nthis may take a bit longer to get rid of the lake effect cloud \nproduction than originally expected. Mostly to partly cloudy \nconditions especially downstream of the lake looks to hang on \ninto Tuesday morning before clearing skies amid a push of drier \nair moves into the area on Tuesday.\n\nDry conditions continue and a slow warming trend will take place\ninto this workweek as the ridging continues to push eastward \ninto the region. Highs in the 50s for Monday through Wednesday\nwith a few locations getting close to the 60 degree mark by\nWednesday afternoon and then highs into the low to mid 60s\nacross the area from Thursday into the weekend. Mornings over\nthe next few days will continue to be crisp with lows right\naround the freezing mark, however any areas with the low to\nmid clouds may stay a couple degrees warmer, so the low forecast\nwill be predicated by the amount of cloud cover that is present\nfor a given location. \n\nStill a quite a bit of uncertainty in the longer term as\nguidance has been quick to dampen the ridging and perhaps bring\nin a disturbance just to the north of the area through the Great\nLakes but this may be too optimistic and progressive in terms \nto what the actual outcome is.",
